      'Support queues swell when return policies read like defense counsel drafted them for a different century. Shoppers do not need Latinate shields; they need to know how many days they have, who pays shipping, and what “unopened” means for their category. Lead the returns page with a five-line summary box: window length, starting trigger such as delivery date versus ship date, eligible condition, refund method and timing range, and exclusions. Put the full legal text below for people who want it. Most buyers never scroll past an honest summary.',
      'Define the clock in plain sentences. “Thirty days from the delivery date shown in your tracking” beats “within thirty (30) days of receipt.” If weekends or holidays affect the cutoff for drop-offs, say so. Ambiguous clocks are ticket factories. Category exceptions should be listed as product-type rules, not buried footnotes. Final-sale swimwear, engraved items, opened audio earbuds, and mattresses with trial periods each need a named row. Link those rows from relevant product pages so shoppers do not discover exclusions after payment.',
      'Explain the steps as a numbered path: start a return in the account, print or scan the label, pack with the order number visible, drop off, then wait for the inspection notice. Screenshots of the portal help more than synonyms for “initiate.” If you require original packaging, state it before purchase on pages that commonly arrive gift-wrapped. Refund economics deserve clarity without cruelty: say whether shipping is refundable, whether restocking fees apply and when, and whether store credit differs from original tender.',
      'Exchanges need their own short section. Inventory availability, price-difference handling, and whether a second shipment starts a new return window are frequent blind spots. Spell them out so agents are not improvising in chat. Translate policy into product-page microcopy near the buy button such as “Free returns within 30 days — final sale on gift cards.” That single line reduces pre-purchase tickets when it matches the returns page exactly. Mismatched snippets are worse than silence because they create exhibits.',
      'Review the top twenty return-reason macros with customer experience each quarter and feed unclear policy phrases back into the rewrite. If agents keep pasting a clarifying sentence, that sentence belongs on the public page. Test comprehension with five outsiders who have never bought from you. Ask them to answer the last day to return, who pays return shipping, and whether an opened box of a sensitive category is allowed. Rewrite until at least four get the answers right without hunting.',
      'Keep marketplace return rules separate when they differ. A direct window of thirty days beside a marketplace fourteen-day auto-approval needs labeled sections. Blended policies create “but your other listing said” disputes that waste time on both sides. Write the damaged-in-transit path separately from buyer’s-remorse returns. Mixing those flows confuses people with broken items who think they must pay return shipping under the remorse rules.',
      'If you use a returns portal vendor, audit their default emails for tone and accuracy. Vendor templates often reintroduce legalese you just removed from the policy page. Brand the portal copy to match your plain-language standard. Holiday extensions need dated announcements with the year included: “Extended returns through January 15 for orders placed after November 1.” Evergreen wording without years creates November confusion the following season.',
      'For international orders, state whether returns ship back to origin, to a local hub, or are not accepted. Cross-border surprise costs dominate negative reviews even when the product delighted. Align chatbot answers with the summary box. Bots that scrape outdated policy URLs undo a careful rewrite overnight. Point bots at the canonical policy URL and retest dialogs after every publish.',
      'Turn recurring return questions into FAQ entries that quote the summary box word-for-word, then link to the full policy. Same vocabulary everywhere is what actually drops ticket volume. Different paraphrases across FAQ, chat macros, and the policy page invite shoppers to argue which version applies.',
      'Measure success with ticket volume tagged to policy confusion, average handle time on returns chats, and refund disputes citing conflicting windows. A readable policy that still produces confusion usually has a presentation problem — bury the summary box less, enlarge the dates, or add the product-page snippet where buyers actually decide.',
      'When you tighten eligibility, announce the change with an effective date and grandfather clearly. Silent tightening feels like a trap and often costs more in chargebacks than the returns you hoped to avoid. Plain language includes plain change management.',
      'Train new agents on annotated screenshots of the summary box answering the five most common questions. Do not start them on the full legal appendix. Competence on the summary prevents improvisation, and improvisation is how plain-language projects die in the inbox.',
      'Revisit the summary after any carrier or portal change that alters timelines or drop-off instructions. Operational changes without copy updates recreate the ticket spikes you thought you fixed, only with new error codes shoppers cannot decode alone.',
