      'Describe what “curated” means operationally. Does the team personalize per quiz answers, rotate fixed themes, or ship identical hero SKUs with a surprise accessory? Shoppers forgive surprises when the rules of surprise are disclosed; they punish brands that advertised personalization but delivered random inventory clearance. Spell out swap and preference tools with screenshots or numbered instructions. “Manage my box” is not enough if the interface is dense. Call out lead times: preference changes lock five days before the bill date. Missed locks are a leading cause of angry public posts.',
      'Inventory realities belong in the copy. If a popular item may sell out mid-cycle and get a substitute, say how substitutes are chosen and whether members can approve them. Silent substitutions feel like bait-and-switch even when contracts allow them. Gift subscriptions need distinct language. Recipients may not know they are on a recurring plan if packaging and email only celebrate the unboxing. Include a discreet card insert and email that explain duration and who controls cancellation.',
      'Pricing modules should contrast subscription versus one-time without dark patterns. Show the math: per-box price, shipping treatment, and what happens to prepaid plans if someone pauses. Prepaid clarity prevents “you stole months” chargebacks. Trial and intro pricing must declare the post-intro price and length. “Then forty-eight dollars every month” with the date of the first full-price charge visible in checkout reduces “I never agreed” disputes. Hide post-intro prices and you invite platform scrutiny.',
      'Align SMS, packaging inserts, and product-page FAQs on the same skip deadline. Mixed dates across surfaces are interpreted as tricks. Choose one chronometer — calendar days before charge — and reuse the same wording everywhere. Monitor cancel-survey free text for phrases like “I thought it was one box” or “too hard to skip.” Those phrases are editorial tickets. Rewrite the module named in the survey spike before you invest in win-back discounts that paper over confusion.',
      'Retention offers after cancel should not rewrite the original promise. A panicked forty percent off that never appears on the join page trains members to cancel for coupons. Prefer extra customization credits or a free skip to discounts that reprice the whole program. Announce content themes early enough for skips to be meaningful. A theme email two days before charge helps nobody who needed five days to decide. Match announcement lead time to your stated skip lock.',
      'Explain what happens to unused credits, paused months, and failed payments without euphemism. Soft language around dunning feels polite in drafts and deceptive in practice when someone is charged after a failed card retry. If alcohol, allergens, or age-restricted goods can appear, put eligibility and identification requirements on the join page. Surprise hazmat or age gates at delivery destroy trust and create expensive reverse logistics.',
      'Community features such as votes and add-on shops need copy that distinguishes optional spend from the core subscription. Members should never wonder whether skipping the box also skips their vote access. When you sunset a plan tier, give written timelines and migration options on both email and the account page. Plan sunsets without copy infrastructure create backlash indistinguishable from surprise billing.',
      'FAQ entries should cover cadence, skips, substitutions, and cancel steps in the order members actually fail them. Pull wording from cancel surveys so the answers sound like the questions people type at midnight. Same vocabulary on the join page, account portal, and FAQ prevents the “which version is real?” arguments that escalate to chargebacks.',
