      'Care icons survive laundry rooms as mysterious hieroglyphs. Customers who ignore them are not lazy — they are busy, and abstract symbols without verbs lose to whatever habit they already have. Plain instructions that fit how people actually clean are what protect products. Rewrite symbols into action sentences ordered by when the customer needs them: first wash or wipe, routine care, what to avoid, storage, and end-of-life notes if relevant. Lead with the action verb: “Machine wash cold, gentle cycle. Lay flat to dry. Do not bleach.”',
      'Prioritize the failure modes that already show up in returns: melted soles, warped lids, faded prints, cracked leather. Put the avoidance line that prevents that failure early, even if it feels negative. “Keep away from open flame” saves more product than “enjoy cozy evenings.” Match reading level to the stressful moment. Care happens at sinks and washing machines, often with wet hands. Short lines, generous punctuation, and no nested clauses win. If a step requires a special detergent, name a category rather than a single brand unless partnership requires it.',
      'Place care copy where it will be seen at the decision to buy and after the unboxing. A product-page care module helps converters who fear maintenance; a hangtag or insert card helps owners. QR codes to a care video help for complex gear, but the first sentence on the insert should still stand alone offline. Differentiate fabric blends and finishes that share a silhouette. A coated jacket and an uncoated twin cannot share care text just because SKUs look alike. Wrong shared care sheets create “I followed the instructions” damage claims that are hard to refuse.',
      'For hard goods, replace fashion laundry language with category verbs: rinse, air-dry thoroughly, oil hinges, tighten bolts seasonally. Seasonal reminder lines such as resealing wood every twelve months in humid climates reduce warranty tickets when climate actually matters. Avoid absolute guarantees wrapped as care advice. “Follow these steps for lifelong performance” can be read as a promise. Prefer “following these steps helps prevent the most common wear issues.” Longevity still depends on use intensity you cannot control.',
      'Test instructions with two people who have never owned the product. Watch whether they skip a step or invent a shortcut. If both invent the same shortcut, either redesign the care path or write an explicit warning against that shortcut. Connect customer-experience macros to care pages. When agents paste the same bleach warning twenty times a week, elevate it on the product page and packaging. Care copy should be a living extract of damage tickets, not a leftover vendor PDF.',
      'Track “damaged — improper care” tags before and after rewrites. Pair that with satisfaction comments mentioning confusion. The goal is fewer preventable damages and fewer arguments about whether the brand explained the rules. Publish care FAQs that answer edge cases — stain types, travel washing, dishwasher safe or not — using the same verbs as the hangtag so owners are not told two different rituals.',
      'Use progressive disclosure for power users: a short default block, then advanced care for conditioning schedules or specialty solvents. Most owners need the short block; enthusiasts will expand. Illustrate irreversible mistakes with a single clear warning icon and sentence, not a collage of scare images. Visual panic reduces comprehension; one strong warning increases compliance.',
      'Synchronize care text across marketplace bullet points and direct modules. Marketplaces that truncate mid-sentence can turn “do not bleach” into something dangerous if a line break falls badly — test truncations. For furniture and durables, include seasonal climate notes without implying universal weather. Humidity, salt air, and direct sun differ by region; invite owners to adjust frequency rather than promising a single calendar for every climate.',
      'When products ship with care booklets in multiple languages, ensure the English product page matches the booklet version number. Mismatched editions are a classic dispute exhibit. After packaging redesigns, photograph the new insert into the product information system within a day of first receipt so digital and physical care stay twins.',
      'For kits with multiple materials, give part-specific care rather than a blended paragraph that averages conflicting needs. A wooden handle and a carbon blade do not share wash instructions. Part-specific lines prevent owners from applying the gentlest rule to everything or the harshest rule to the most delicate piece.',
