      'Separate line items when carts mix in-stock and delayed SKUs. Tell shoppers whether you split shipments by default and how shipping fees apply to each leg. Surprise freight on the delayed half creates tickets that look like bait. On confirmation emails, restate the status, window, and cancel path. Many buyers skim product pages; the confirmation is a second chance to align memory with reality. Use the same date format everywhere to avoid regional misreads between day-month and month-day conventions.',
      'Product-page badges should link to a short FAQ covering production stage, why dates are estimates, and what happens if a component supplier misses. Transparency about manufacturing beats vague “high demand” mystique. Train ads and influencers to include the ship window when featuring preorder SKUs. Paid creative that looks like “ships tomorrow” while the page says August is an avoidable self-own that support cannot walk back with charm.',
