      'Literal translation preserves words and loses shoppers. A brand voice that sounds witty in English can sound rude, childish, or nonsensical in another market if you port idioms unchanged. Localization is editorial work with bilingual judgment, not a swap of strings. Start with a voice brief per language: formality level, banned idioms, preferred second person, and how you handle humor. Include three sample paragraphs that local editors agree sound like the brand. Without that north star, freelancers invent parallel brands that share a logo and little else.',
      'Separate legal and measurement requirements from voice. Units, warranty jurisdictions, and required consumer notices must change by market even when tone stays warm. Build a compliance checklist that sits beside the voice brief so neither side overwrites the other during rushed launches. Translate benefits, then re-prove them with market-relevant proof. A “perfect for college dorms” line may need a different living-situation metaphor abroad. Keep the product fact identical; swap the lifestyle frame so it rings true locally without inventing new performance claims.',
      'Maintain a glossary of product nouns, finish names, and trademarked terms. Uncontrolled synonyms across locales break search facets and make review translation messy. Lock color names carefully — poetic English finishes often need stable local trademarks. Avoid one-click machine translation for product pages that carry claims. Machine drafts can accelerate first passes for long catalogs if a human editor with category fluency reviews every claim sentence. Unreviewed machine output is how “organic” and “recyclable” land incorrectly and create regulatory exposure.',
      'Adapt length expectations. Some languages inflate character counts and break mobile cards designed around English titles. Give local editors permission to rewrite titles for fit rather than forcing overcrowded truncations that hide the differentiating attribute. Review currency, shipping, and return modules as first-class copy, not boilerplate. Crossing borders changes the friction shoppers care about. Recycled United States returns copy in a market with different consumer rights reads tone-deaf and sometimes unlawful.',
      'Pilot with top sellers and a small paid test in the new locale before bulk-publishing. Compare conversion and support themes against the source language. Language issues often surface as “unclear sizing” tickets even when the root is a mistranslated measurement. Create a feedback loop from local customer-experience teams into the glossary monthly. Agents hear which phrases confuse native speakers. Treat those notes as editorial defects, not as customer ignorance to be trained away.',
      'Protect brand wit selectively. Humor that depends on English wordplay should be replaced with locally funny equivalents, not explained. Forced jokes signal “translated marketing,” which erodes premium positioning. When catalogs are large, rewrite in batches with shared glossaries and sample approved product pages so each locale stays consonant. Machine assistance can normalize length and structure while editors keep claims and humor native to the market.',
      'Hire reviewers who shop the category in-market, not only linguists who know grammar. Category fluency catches absurd care advice and wrong rival-product assumptions that pure language review misses. Decide early whether price-psychology phrases fit local norms. Some markets find certain urgency idioms crass; voice preservation includes knowing which brand muscles to flex and which to leave in the source market.',
      'Keep untranslatable product names intentional. If the trademark stays English, give a local descriptor beside it on first mention so search and clarity both win. Align localized ads with localized product pages before scaling. Nothing burns trust like a carefully localized ad landing on English leftovers for returns and shipping modules.',
      'Document dialect choices — for example European versus Latin American Spanish — in the brief and stick to them across titles, bots, and emails. Mixed dialects read as careless even when each sentence is locally valid somewhere. Consistency inside a market beats linguistic perfection that switches dialects mid-catalog.',
      'Budget time for screenshot reviews of checkout and empty states, not only hero descriptions. Microcopy failures in localized filters and error messages create abandonments that look like “market fit” problems in a dashboard. Voice includes the small words at the moments of friction.',
      'When legal requires additional notices, place them near the relevant decision without letting them flatten the brand voice elsewhere. Design can isolate required text; writers should not respond by making the entire page sterile. Separation of concerns keeps compliance and personality from destroying each other.',
      'Revisit localization after major product redesigns in the source language. Source pages change; locales rot when nobody triggers a re-localization ticket. Tie locale updates to the same change-control list as the source catalog so markets do not quietly diverge for six months.',
      'Success looks like local customers describing your brand the way your brief hoped — and support tickets that sound like product issues rather than translation issues. If agents keep clarifying language, the localization is unfinished regardless of how many strings show as “done” in a spreadsheet.',
      'Celebrate local editors who catch source errors, not only translation issues. A market launch often reveals ambiguities in the original language; feeding those findings back improves the mothership catalog for everyone.',
