      'Catalog microcopy is the quiet guide rail between search intent and product cards. When filter labels sound like internal taxonomy and empty states shrug with “no results,” shoppers leave even though inventory exists one facet away. Name filters for shopper language, not product-information-system field names. “Waist” beats an internal attribute code. “Water-resistant” beats a feature flag label. Run a handful of customer interviews or mine reviews for synonyms before renaming facets across the site, then lock the chosen terms in a glossary merchandising cannot casually reinvent.',
      'Sort options need outcome language. Replace opaque defaults when algorithms mix popularity and margin. Prefer clear labels such as “Most popular,” “Newest,” and “Price: low to high.” If “Recommended” is personalized, say “Recommended for you” so the shifting order feels intentional rather than buggy. Explain multi-select versus single-select near dense filters. A six-word hint such as “Select multiple brands” prevents false empty states caused by exclusive clicks. Hints belong adjacent to the control, not only in a distant help article nobody opens mid-browse.',
      'Empty states should diagnose and recover. “No hiking boots in size 42 and red” plus buttons to clear color or clear size outperforms a lonely “No products found.” Show the active filters in the message so the diagnosis is obvious on mobile. Offer constructive next steps when inventory truly is gone: notify-me, related categories, or recently viewed. Empty states that only apologize waste the session. Ones that route to adjacent demand capture email without feeling like a dead end.',
      'Keep applied-filter chips readable. Truncated chip text forces memory work. Prefer wrapping or a “+2 more” pattern with an expandable list over cryptic ellipsis on the primary constraint. Zero-result messaging for site search differs from facet empties. For search, suggest corrected spellings and popular queries. For facets, emphasize clearing constraints. Reusing one template for both frustrates two different mental models and makes the catalog feel broken twice.',
      'Localize sort and filter labels with the same glossary discipline as product pages. A half-translated facet set signals an unfinished market launch and erodes trust before anyone opens a product. Instrument filter paths that most often end empty. High empty rates on a popular pair such as size and color may indicate inventory gaps worth merchandising differently — or a mislabeled facet that excludes too much inventory from view.',
      'Review microcopy in accessibility pass-throughs: announce filter changes to screen readers with polite, brief updates such as “twenty-four products shown” instead of silent grid swaps. Clarity in live regions is part of the same writing job as the visible labels. When product cards pull titles into filtered grids, make sure the visible title still carries size or color cues the filters promised. Keep those cues upfront so cards match the facet story shoppers just selected.',
      'Count labels on filters when helpful: “Wool (24)” sets expectations before the click. Hide zero-count options or mark them unavailable instead of letting people enter guaranteed empty states. Write error copy for partial failures such as a facet service timeout differently from true empties. “Filters temporarily unavailable — showing all” prevents shoppers from thinking your catalog shrank to zero during a brief outage.',
      'Merchandise seasonal filters with explicit dates in admin documentation so “Holiday” does not linger into March. Stale seasonal facets create empties that feel like stockouts and teach people to distrust filters generally. For business-to-business storefronts, add industry-standard codes in filter synonyms while keeping the visible label human. Dual taxonomy supports purchasing agents without alienating casual browsers who need plain language.',
      'A/B empty-state illustrations only after the text recovers well. Pretty art on a hopeless message is decoration; clear recovery actions are the product. Test empty states with real frustrating combinations from analytics, not only with designer-chosen scenarios that never occur in production traffic.',
      'When introducing a new facet, publish a one-line definition on first use for ambiguous terms such as “performance fit” or “full grain.” Undefined facets generate empties born of misunderstanding rather than inventory. Definitions can disappear after a launch window once education has happened.',
      'Coordinate sort defaults with merchandising goals transparently. If the default is not “newest,” say what it optimizes for in internal docs and ensure the public label does not lie. Misleading sort names create the same distrust as misleading scarcity badges.',
      'After search-engine optimizations change titles en masse, recheck truncated cards under active filters. Title experiments that help search can hurt facet comprehension if they push size and color beyond the visible character budget. Microcopy and titles share one interface budget.',
      'Make empty-state ownership clear: experience design owns patterns, merchandising owns facet names, engineering owns counts. Ambiguous ownership is how “temporary” vague copy becomes permanent furniture on the highest-traffic templates in the store.',
      'Ship filter microcopy changes behind a short QA checklist: counts correct, multi-select hints visible, empty diagnosis accurate, and screen-reader announcements non-spammy. Microcopy is code-adjacent; treat releases accordingly.',
