      'Shoppers arrive on product pages already comparing. They have three tabs open, a spreadsheet of features, or a friend who owns the rival brand. Pretending you have no competition makes the page feel evasive. Attacking rivals by name with loaded adjectives creates legal risk and makes you look insecure. Fair comparison copy gives decision criteria without smearing anyone, which is safer and more persuasive for careful buyers who have been burned by hype before.',
      'Frame comparisons around attributes shoppers can verify, not vibes. "Our drip tray holds 300 ml" and "designed for counters under 40 cm deep" help more than "beats Brand X in performance." When you must mention a category alternative—vacuum versus steam mop, cartridge versus tank filter—describe trade-offs with numbers, maintenance burden, noise, and ideal use cases. Let the shopper apply those criteria to whatever else they are considering in other tabs.',
      'Build a best-if and skip-if pair near the fold. Best-if sentences welcome the right buyer: people who cook daily, renters who cannot drill into walls, kids\' rooms needing low-VOC finishes. Skip-if sentences politely redirect wrong fits: no outdoor use, not for commercial kitchens, incompatible with certain phone models. Redirecting bad fits prevents angry reviews and chargebacks that cost more than the conversion you temporarily gained by overstating breadth.',
      'Comparison tables work when rows are measurable. Columns can be your own model variants rather than competitor logos, which often avoids trademark and accuracy headaches. Shoppers still feel guided because they see which SKU owns which job. If you include competitor columns, stick to publicly available specs, cite sources where practical, and date-stamp the table so outdated claims do not haunt you after a rival ships a redesign mid-season.',
      'Never invent rival weaknesses. Support tickets and forum screenshots about competitor failures are not your advertising assets. Stick to your lab measurements, warranty length, included accessories, and response-time commitments. Absolute claims about another company\'s durability, ethics, or "real" materials invite rebuttal letters from their counsel and comment wars you cannot win inside a product description field.',
      'Tone matters as much as facts. "Unlike cheap imports that peel" insults an entire supply chain and many past customers who bought entry models from you or others. Prefer language like "we specify bonded seams rated to a stated wash-cycle count; check manufacturer data if you need heavier duty." Professional restraint reads confident. Snark reads like compensation for a thin differentiator, and it rarely ages well in screenshots.',
      'Place comparison modules where decisions stall—after the hero benefit and before Add to Cart, or as an expandable section titled around common options. Do not bury the only honest limitations in an accordion nobody opens while putting glowing superiority language above the fold. Disclosure that arrives after purchase is how disputes start and how brands that were trusted for fairness lose that reputation in a single viral post.',
      'Train writers to avoid coded insults: phrases about what "real professionals" choose, what "serious cooks" never buy, or dismissals of "those gimmicks." Those lines trash competitors without naming them and still alienate careful shoppers who dislike status pressure. Replace that energy with scenario matching: who thrives with this product, who should pick another class of tool, and why the trade-off exists in the real world.',
      'Align ads and comparison copy. A paid search ad that claims supremacy against a named rival will collide with a measured product page and create disappointment at the exact moment trust matters. Keep paid claims milder than page claims, not louder. The landing page should feel like clarification of the ad, not a contradiction that makes the shopper question every other sentence on the page.',
      'For regulated categories—supplements, infant goods, financial tools—have compliance review comparison language early. Labels like clinically preferred, doctor recommended, and number-one rated are comparison statements that usually need substantiation files you can produce on request. When you cannot defend a ranking adjective, delete it. Silence is better than a claim that collapses under a complaint or a platform takedown.',
      'Refresh comparison blocks when you change materials, drop a feature, or change warranty terms. Nothing erodes trust faster than a table that still trumpets a stainless insert you removed two seasons ago. Assign merchandising ownership so comparisons expire with bill-of-materials changes, and include comparison modules in the same quality checklist as specs, images, and shipping notes before a relaunch.',
      'After rewriting comparison sections, a brief meta pass helps search snippets stay aligned with the page\'s decision criteria without stuffing rival brand names into titles. Focus titles and descriptions on the shopper\'s choice job—quiet blender for apartments, wide-calf boots with true measurements—not on tearing someone else\'s product down in search results where you do not control the rebuttal.',
      'Used well, comparison copy shortens consideration and improves post-purchase satisfaction because buyers self-selected. Used poorly, it becomes competitive theater that legal has to mop up later. Aim for the former: clear criteria, dated facts, professional tone, and an open invitation for the wrong customer to walk away before they pay and regret it.',
