      'Bundles fail when shoppers invent contents that were never promised. Words like starter kit, complete set, and everything you need invite fantasies. The cure is inventory-style prose: name each included unit, its quantity, and its model or size, then state what sits outside the box. Precision feels less romantic in marketing meetings and far more defensive when a dispute examiner asks what the customer could reasonably expect from the listing.',
      'Lead with the job the bundle completes. Two weeks of travel skincare for carry-on limits, or a first-apartment coffee station without buying pieces separately, orients the reader before the packing list. Once purpose is clear, itemized line items read as proof rather than a parts catalog for its own sake. Shoppers who only skim still leave knowing whether this kit matches the weekend or the problem they brought to the page.',
      'Use a scannable inclusions list—bullets or a short table—above longer narrative. Mobile buyers will not excavate a paragraph to discover whether batteries ship in the box. Put exclusions such as batteries not included and tool sold separately on their own lines with the same visual weight as included items. Visual hierarchy that buries exclusions is how you win a cart click and lose the chargeback argument later.',
      'Clarify interchangeability and randomness. If the bundle contains a medium refill that does not fit the large dispenser you also sell, say so near the list. If fragrance variants ship as a random assortment, disclose the selection method. Surprise SKUs inside kits generate refund tags that look identical to damaged-item refunds in reporting but are really expectation failures you could have prevented with one honest sentence.',
      'Price justification belongs in savings language only when the math is auditable. Claiming a savings amount versus buying separately requires an on-page or linked reference to solo prices that shoppers can still find. Fake value percentages that drift weekly train skepticism. If you cannot keep solo prices stable, drop the savings claim and sell convenience, curation, and packaging instead—stories that do not require a spreadsheet to remain true.',
      'Audience filters reduce mismatch. A new-homeowner toolkit that includes drywall anchors but no drill should say it assumes a drill you already own. Gift bundles should note allergens, alcohol content, or gender-neutral positioning when those objections drive holiday returns. The kinder thing is a clear mismatch sentence, not a surprise that forces a return during peak season when shipping is slow and emotions run high.',
      'Photography must match the written inventory. Hero images that show six bottles when the kit ships four become litigation exhibits. Shoot the exact configuration that leaves the warehouse, and when SKU photography lags behind a kit redesign, temporarily use a plain pack-out photo rather than an outdated lifestyle composite that overstates contents. Writers should have veto power when creative wants a prettier but false frame.',
      'Explain substitution rules for out-of-stock components before the customer pays. Will you delay shipment, swap an equal-value item, or cancel the line and refund part of the order? Write the policy in the description and surface it again at checkout when kits are volatile. Quiet substitutions feel like bait even when the financial value is fair, because control over what arrives was removed without consent.',
      'Care and setup instructions for kits should sequence the unboxing: open pouch A first, charge module B overnight, discard silica sachets before first use. Bundles with many small parts generate missing-piece claims that careful sequences, labeled pouches, and a photo of the open box reduce. If a part is spare or decorative, label it so it is not mistaken for a required fastener customers think they lost.',
