    title: 'How to Write Size Guides That Cut Wrong-Size Orders',
    description:
      'Create clear, measurable size guide copy so shoppers pick the right fit the first time and returns drop.',
    date: '2025-12-17',
    readTime: '8 min',
    toolPath: '/tools/product-faq-generator',
    toolLabel: 'Product FAQ Generator',
    content: [
      'Wrong-size orders are rarely mysteries. Shoppers guessed from vanity sizing, ignored inches, or followed a generic small-medium-large chart that never matched your pattern block. Size-guide copy that earns its space translates measurements into decisions, admits manufacturing variance, and tells people when to size up without shaming them for body shape or past brand loyalty.',
      'Always publish units and methods. State whether waist numbers are garment measurements or body measurements, whether the tape sits at the navel or the natural waist, and whether shoes use EU, UK, or US lasts. A one-sentence note on how you measure prevents the classic dispute where both parties swear their tape is correct and neither can prove the listing explained the method.',
      'Prefer ranges over single fantasy numbers when tolerance exists. A chest range of a few centimeters paired with a note that finished garments may vary slightly is honest. Perfect-pixel precision on stretch fabrics becomes a refund reason when a customer expects bespoke accuracy from an affordable tee. Honesty about variance is not weakness; it is how you set a fair bar for "as described."',
      'Model notes should include height, usual size in other brands when known, and the size worn in your brand. Saying Maya is a certain height, usually wears a stated EU size, and is wearing that same size here helps more than "model wears small." If the model sized up for an oversized look, say that explicitly so shoppers do not assume the cut runs large across the board.',
      'Fit vocabulary needs definitions. Slim, tailored, relaxed, and oversized mean different things across brands and even across your own categories. Dedicate one line each that ties the word to ease and shape, such as how much room sits at the hip. Without definitions, adjectives are decoration that marketing likes and support cannot defend when a customer expected something else.',
      'Between-size guidance should be specific to fabric. For rigid denim, you might advise choosing the larger size and expecting a little give after a few wears. For high-stretch knits, choosing the smaller size may produce a closer look. Blanket "always size up" advice across categories creates new error patterns while sounding helpful in a workshop that never looked at return tags.',
      'Place the guide where hesitation happens—near the size selector, not only on a distant footer page. Inline expanders that show the chart without leaving the product page outperform hidden policy links that feel like homework. Still keep a durable size-guide URL for search visibility and for support agents who need a stable link to paste into email replies.',
      'International charts need dual units and clear conversion caveats. Do not silently convert and round differently by category; a dress chart that rounds up while a jacket chart rounds down will confuse loyal customers who buy both. Document rounding rules once in your internal style notes and apply them everywhere so conversion math does not become tribal knowledge.',
      'Kids and shoe sizing deserve special caution because growth and width matter as much as length. Include age bands only as rough signals, and foreground foot length in millimeters when you can. Width options should be described physically—narrow, regular, wide—with what that means at the ball of the foot, not only letter codes that vary by factory.',
      'Connect returns data to the guide every month. If too-small-in-shoulders spikes for a blazer style, add a shoulder measurement row or a note about structured pads. Guides that never change while return reasons evolve are abandoned assets that look complete in the CMS and fail quietly in the warehouse returns bay.',
      'Photograph at least one flat lay or on-body shot with a tape or known object for hard goods where dimensions dominate—rugs, shelves, laptop sleeves, storage bins. Pair the visual with the exact number stated in the guide so shoppers can reconcile photo scale against furniture and rooms they already own without guessing from lifestyle crop alone.',
      'For apparel brands with many factories, avoid one global chart if blocks differ by collection. Label charts by fabric family or collection name. A single misleading master chart can wipe out conversion gains from prettier photos and better ads because the wrong size still arrives and the customer blames the brand, not the missing chart split.',
      'Pair the guide with two or three FAQs born from real tickets: pre-wash shrink expectations, swapping sizes after delivery, and how to measure yourself without a soft tape. Those answers belong next to the chart, not only in a distant help center. When sizing questions keep landing in chat, fold them into an on-page FAQ module so shoppers decide before they buy—optionally drafting FAQ candidates from your ticket themes, then editing every number against the live chart.',
      'Create a customer-facing measuring video transcript next to the chart when returns still spike after a clear table ships. Spoken steps for finding the natural waist or tracing a foot on paper help people who freeze at numbers. Keep the transcript aligned with the written method so video and text cannot disagree on where the tape sits.',
      'Finally, train associates and chat bots on the same chart language. If live chat invents a different sizing rule than the page, you teach customers to distrust both. Publish an internal cheat sheet that quotes the live guide verbatim and update it whenever the guide changes, including during flash drops when new colorways reuse old blocks.',
    ],
