      'Product pages fail in the gap between what the camera sells and what the paragraph promises. A photo can imply room-filling light while the specs list a modest lumen count. Copy can claim dishwasher safety while lifestyle shots show a hand-washed glaze that looks fragile. Alignment is not a mood-board exercise; it is a shared brief with constraints both crafts honor before either team spends a full day on the wrong story.',
      'Start briefs before either team drafts final work. List mandatory truths: exact colorway shipping now, accessories included in frame, surfaces the product will actually sit on, and claims that must appear in text because photos cannot prove them—certifications, watt hours, allergens, warranty months. Photographers should know banned implications the same way writers know banned adjectives, and both lists should live in one document.',
      'Shot lists keyed to benefits keep supporting imagery honest. If copy says the bottle fits a bike cage, shoot the bottle in that cage with measurable clearance visible. If copy highlights quiet motors, do not rely on a serene kitchen image alone—consider a secondary graphic or caption stating tested ranges so silence is not merely vibed into existence without evidence.',
      'Sequence galleries to match scroll narrative. Lead with identity and scale, then a detail of the differentiator the first paragraph names, then context of use, then packaging or in-hand size. Random beauty dumps force writers to apologize in FAQs for what images oversold. Order is part of truth-telling when shoppers never read past the third thumbnail on a phone.',
      'Color management is a joint problem. Writers should name colors as they appear under agreed lighting, not as marketing poetry alone. A sage label next to a photo that reads olive on most phones becomes a return reason. Include a reference card in at least one image when color accuracy drives returns in the category, and update names when dyes shift between production lots.',
      'Props are claims. A photo featuring food beside a cleaner implies a use case. If the formula is not food-surface safe, remove the lemons or add an unmistakable caption. Prop stylists need a yes-or-no list updated when formulations change, and writers should review styled frames with the same seriousness they review chemical claims in body copy.',
      'Human models create size stories even when nobody intends it. Coordinate clothing size notes in captions with the size guide the writer maintains. A cropped jacket photo that hides sleeve length while copy brags about full coverage is a quiet contradiction shoppers feel even if they cannot articulate why they hesitate. Show the hem when the hem is the claim.',
      'Video and stills must share continuity. If a demo video shows a detachable lid but seasonal packaging now seals differently, either reshoot or mute that chapter with an on-screen note. Writers should never be asked to word around a false demo indefinitely; that request is how brand voice becomes a shield for outdated media instead of a carrier of current truth.',
      'Create a shared annotation layer for each thumbnail: one sentence of approved caption material and one risk flag, such as showing an optional stand sold separately. This artifact survives freelancer turnover better than chat history. Store it next to the asset in your DAM or merchandising sheet so later campaigns do not recycle a risky frame without the caption it required.',
      'Review together, not in serial isolation. A short sync where writer and photographer open the staged product page catches mismatches faster than separate approvals that each assume the other craft is fine. Invite merchandising only after the pair agrees the story is consistent; early crowd feedback often debates style while the factual mismatch sits unnoticed.',
      'When budget forces stock or supplier photos, writers inherit stricter duties: disclose flattering angles, avoid claiming finishes not visible, and push for one honest pack-out image as soon as inventory allows. Borrowed imagery is rent; owned alignment is equity. Note temporary media status internally so relaunches swap in truthful photography instead of pretending the stock shot is permanent.',
      'Measure disputes tagged as visual mismatch—wrong color, missing part shown, scale confusion—and review those SKUs as a creative pair. Punishing only the writer when the photo lied teaches the wrong lesson and leaves the camera free to oversell again. Joint ownership over product-page truth keeps both crafts proud of the page that ships and accountable for the reviews that follow.',
      'When writers need faster first drafts after a shoot, generate body copy from the annotated shot list and fact sheet rather than from memory of the studio day. The draft should name only what appears or what ops confirmed, then pass through a photographer check for visual overclaim before publish. That small loop closes more refund tags than another round of adjective polishing.',
      'Budget time for a mismatch triage after major launches. Pull the first fifty reviews mentioning photo or color and sit writer plus photographer together to decide whether copy, imagery, or both must change. Rapid joint triage beats separate tickets that fix adjectives while leaving the misleading lifestyle crop in the hero slot.',
