      'Reviews already contain the vocabulary your listings lack. Buyers describe squeaks that vanish after a week, straps that survived a move, and colors that looked brighter in person. Mining that language for product-page proof beats inventing marketer poetry—provided you stay faithful to what people actually wrote and to platform rules about testimonials, endorsements, and how ratings must be displayed.',
      'Cluster reviews by theme before you quote anyone. Fit accuracy, setup time, durability after months, and gift reception are common clusters. For each theme, select one short authentic phrase and, separately, a paraphrased insight you state as a general benefit only if multiple reviews support it. One poetic outlier is not a trend, and treating it as one is how proof modules become fiction with better punctuation.',
      'Attribute carefully. Use first names and locations only when your review platform\'s terms and privacy policy allow. Never invent a persona for emotional effect. When in doubt, present aggregate insight—many buyers mention easy wipe-downs—without fake characters. Fabricated people are a shortcut that collapses the moment someone asks for the original review URL.',
      'Balance is credibility. A proof module that only shows five-star raves while the widget below shows a middling average feels curated into fiction. Pair strengths with an honest limitation drawn from reviews, such as running warm with advice to size down, and make sure that limitation also appears in your size guide or FAQ so proof and guidance agree.',
      'Do not alter meaning when you tighten quotes. Ellipses that remove caveats turn praise into deception. Prefer shorter true excerpts over Frankenstein testimonials stitched from multiple authors. Editors who treat reviews like raw material for slogans will eventually ship a slogan a customer can prove they never said.',
      'Video and photo reviews deserve captions that match what is shown. If a UGC clip demonstrates muddy trail use, do not caption it as formalwear approved. Invite creators with clear briefs so their proof aligns with claims you will put in text, and reject beautiful clips that imply uses your product cannot safely support.',
      'Compliance categories such as supplements, cosmetics, and finance need extra filters: testimonials about curing conditions or guaranteed returns often violate advertising standards even if a customer typed them enthusiastically. Keep those out of merchandising modules; leave raw widgets unedited when platform rules require it, and train merchants not to elevate risky praise into headline copy.',
      'Refresh modules when the product changes. Quoting praise about a zipper brand you no longer use is a factual error dressed as social proof. Tag proof snippets with production era or date range when possible, and retire them when bills of materials change. Old love letters about discontinued materials are liabilities.',
      'Turn negative themes into FAQ and copy upgrades rather than only hiding them. If twenty people struggled with the lid, improve the lid explanation and the packing insert. Proof and problem-solving reinforce each other; ignoring pain themes while amplifying joy themes teaches attentive shoppers that you only listen selectively.',
      'Place proof near the decisions it supports: durability quotes beside warranty mentions, fit quotes beside size selectors, gift quotes near gift-wrap offers. A graveyard of testimonials at the footer is weaker than contextual reinforcement where hesitation happens. Layout is part of ethical persuasion when it helps people find the evidence for the doubt they already have.',
      'Staff responses to reviews are separate from mining them, but public replies that clarify care tips can later inspire FAQ entries. Close the loop so social learning becomes structured page content. A reply that unlocks a better use pattern is wasted if it only lives in a review thread and never reaches the product page most buyers read first.',
      'Measure whether proof modules change conversion on SKUs with enough traffic, and watch for increases in policy complaints about misleading endorsements. Ethical proof raises confidence; inflated proof raises liability. Stay on the ethical side of that line even when a competitor\'s page looks louder, because louder fake proof is not a durable strategy.',
      'When teams rewrite descriptions after a review surge, pull the top phrases by frequency, map them to claims you can still verify, and only then draft body copy that echoes buyer language without inventing degrees of praise. Generators can help arrange those verified phrases into readable paragraphs, but humans must reject any sentence that intensifies a review beyond what the reviewer wrote.',
      'Obtain permissions when you move a review outside its original platform into ads, packaging, or email. On-site widgets may allow display under platform terms; off-site reuse often needs broader consent. Mixing those rules is how a wholesome proof project becomes a rights problem. Keep a permissions log beside your proof library.',
      'Watch for incentivized reviews bleeding into proof modules. If someone received a discount for feedback, many jurisdictions require disclosure, and elevating that quote into a hero proof strip without context is risky. Prefer organic themes with enough volume that no single incentivized voice carries the module.',
