      'Collection pages are often an afterthought: a grid of products under a two-sentence blurb written the day the store launched. Shoppers landing from category searches need orientation—who this assortment is for, how to choose within it, and what quality bar unifies the tiles. Without that guidance, they bounce to a competitor who explains the shelf, even if your products are stronger.',
      'Write a purpose statement above the grid. Travel packing cubes sized by weekend, week-long, and carry-on-only trips immediately filters readers. Avoid circular fluff that welcomes people to a packing-cube collection that is home of packing cubes. Say the job, say the spread of solutions, and let the grid prove you merchandised intentionally.',
      'Provide a decision framework of three questions maximum: material preference, size or capacity, and budget tier if relevant. Link each question to filtered views or to merchandising cards when your theme supports them. Education that cannot connect to navigation is incomplete; shoppers learn and then have nowhere to go with the learning.',
      'SEO introductions can sit below the fold or in expandable sections so they do not push products down endlessly on mobile. Search engines can still parse unique category explanations while humans see products first. Keyword density without helpful structure still fails both parties, and endless walls of text above the first row look like ranking theater.',
      'Highlight differentiators of the assortment, not of every SKU. Saying all shells carry a stated approval or every knife uses full-tang construction sets a floor. Leave SKU-level romance to product pages. Collection copy that tries to sell every tile individually becomes noise that slows choice instead of accelerating it.',
