      'Search engines treat product pages like tiny documents fighting for attention inside a crowded catalog. When titles repeat across colorways, variants share near-blank copy, or metas look identical, crawlers spend budget poorly and shoppers bounce before they understand the offer. The costliest ecommerce SEO mistakes on listings are seldom dramatic penalties; they are quiet operational habits that accumulate across hundreds of SKUs until organic growth stalls for reasons no single weekly dashboard explains clearly.',
      'Duplicate meta titles remain the classic trap. A merchant launches twelve candle scents under one template that only appends the scent name at the end, then watches mobile search snippets truncate before the differentiator appears. Search systems often consolidate near-duplicates and emphasize one URL while siblings languish. Write distinct titles that lead with the attribute people actually type — scent, size, material, capacity — and place the brand near the end unless branded demand clearly dominates your query mix.',
      'Thin variant pages underperform more than teams expect. Minting a URL for every color while leaving the body as "same great product in a new shade" signals emptiness to algorithms and to humans. Either consolidate variants beneath a parent with purposeful swatch notes, or give each indexed color page a real paragraph about finish indoors versus outdoors and how dye lots can drift. Empty pages invite soft-404 treatment over time and teach shoppers that clicking deeper yields nothing.',
      'Keyword stuffing in the opening sentence still survives in catalogs migrated from older platforms. Cramming every synonym into a breathless first line reads machine-made and trains people to skim past you toward a clearer competitor. Prefer one primary phrase in the H1, then let secondary phrases appear naturally inside bullets, alt text, and FAQ questions customers already ask support. Density theater is not strategy when rivals publish measurable facts with calmer grammar.',
      'Missing or generic image alt text fails accessibility and forfeits image-search discovery. Values like "product photo" or leftover camera filenames teach assistive technology nothing useful. Describe what is observable: angle, colorway, visible pack contents, and a scale cue such as a hand, ruler, or model height. Keep alt text factual. Persist persuasion for captions and the description body, where claims can sit beside constraints instead of posing as neutral image labels.',
      'Collection pages that cannibalize product URLs scramble rankings and analytics. When a broad collection targets the same head terms as your hero SKUs, individual product pages can stall even if they convert better. Assign roles on purpose: collections guide browse-and-filter intent with assortment language, while products carry the specifications, offers, and reviews required for a single purchase decision. Overlap without differentiation is how well-intentioned IA becomes a ranking cage match.',
      'Orphan listings — purchasable products with no path from collections, guides, or related rails — crawl slowly and convert poorly because navigation hides them from humans too. Compare crawls against sitemaps monthly. Every live SKU deserves at least one obvious navigable route plus a contextual cross-link that is more intentional than a generic recommendation widget filled with leftovers from unrelated seasons.',
      'Structured data that contradicts visible copy quietly erodes rich-result eligibility and customer trust. Price, availability, and review aggregates in schema must match what the shopper sees today, not last week\'s feed. Stale in-stock flags during backorders, or star counts that ignore removals, create support load and search-feature losses. Treat schema like any other published claim merchandising must maintain after promotions and inventory swings.',
      'Canonical tags aimed at the wrong parent confuse color and size variants for years. Choose a policy once — typically the default shoppable variant or a consolidated parent — and document it for anyone empowered to create products. Freelancers inventing private canonical rules mid-launch are a frequent cause of mysterious ranking drops immediately after catalog expansions that looked operationally successful.',
      'Faceted navigation can explode into endless thin URLs when every filter combination becomes indexable. Color plus size plus price plus sort order is crawl debt, not a content strategy. Noindex or canonicalize non-strategic combinations, and invest unique explanatory copy only in filter landings you truly intend to rank, such as a curated wide-calf assortment with sizing guidance your parent collection cannot cover.',
