    toolLabel: 'Product Description Generator',
    content: [
      'Most product decisions now begin on a phone screen roughly the width of a credit card. Copy written for wide desktop canvases — long openings, sidebar trust stacks, comparison tables that need sideways scroll — collapses into frustration. Mobile-first product copy means prioritizing what must be understood in the first two thumb-scrolls, then allowing depth for the shoppers willing to keep moving.',
      'Title and price must survive one glance. If the title wraps three times and hides the variant name below the fold, people add the wrong size. Prefer concise titles and surface the selected variant prominently in interface copy, not only inside a dropdown that requires extra taps. Clarity at the top is refund prevention as much as conversion craft.',
      'Lead with a two-line benefit-plus-proof block. Pair a feel or outcome claim with a concrete proof such as fabric weight, a lab rating, or a quoted customer outcome. Mobile readers rarely finish a seven-sentence overture before hunting for shipping promises or size charts. Earn the right to storytelling after you earn comprehension.',
      'Bullets beat paragraphs for early decision facts on small screens. Cap the first cluster near five items: key specs, inclusions, primary constraint, shipping or lead time, and return window. Nest richer narrative lower for curious buyers. Scanning is not a failure mode; it is the default mode of shoppers standing in line or riding transit.',
      'Tap targets for size guides and FAQs need text that looks actionable. See-size-chart links buried in gray body copy lose mobile users who will guess instead. Make the path obvious and load charts without forcing pinch-zoom on raster images. Guessed sizes are expensive; linked charts are cheap.',
      'Avoid horizontal tables for core specifications. Convert them into stacked definition pairs with the label on one line and the value on the next. Shoppers abandon comparison grids that require landscape mode while commuting. If a table cannot be stacked cleanly, it may not belong above the fold at all.',
      'Truncate competing promotions. Stacked free-gift banners, app-download prompts, and newsletter popups push product truth below the first fold. If marketing insists on a banner, limit it to one truthful line and keep product identity visible. Attention is finite; treat it like inventory.',
      'Review snippets near the buy button help on mobile only when they are short and specific. A note that a fit runs true to size and that a reviewer ordered a usual medium helps. Long unfocused testimonials delay action without adding confidence. Save essays for the reviews tab.',
      'Lobby for readable theme typography. Elegant desktop type can become microscopic on phones. Copywriters rarely own CSS, yet they can refuse ALL CAPS blocks that shout poorly on bright screens and can escalate contrast failures discovered during dark-mode checks. Illegible disclaimers are disclaimers that never happened.',
      'Test PDPs on cellular data, not only café Wi-Fi. Heavy carousels that delay text mean carefully written openings never appear before bounce. Prioritize text content in performance reviews. Offline or low-signal loads may show HTML while images fail — another reason essentials belong in text, not only in overlays.',
      'Sticky add-to-cart bars must not cover required footnotes or size-chart links. Test with one-handed reach and larger accessibility text settings. If sticky UI wins against copy, purchases may rise while complaints rise faster. Wallet shortcuts like Shop Pay also jump people forward; incomplete expectations on the PDP become tickets you cannot intercept mid-checkout.',
      'Carousels that auto-rotate text overlays are hostile on mobile. Prefer static captions under images. Shoppers reading materials callouts hate chasing moving slides on a jostling commute. Motion should demonstrate the product, not obscure the words that prevent returns.',
      'Pre-publish checklist: title readable without horizontal scroll; first screen answers what it is and who it is for; size and shipping links visible; top facts in bullets; no essential claim trapped in hover states that phones lack; irreversible facts such as final-sale status, made-to-order lead times, and allergen warnings high enough that interrupted shoppers still see them. Write assuming interruption, because interruption is the medium.',
      'Run informal mobile recording sessions with real customers when you can, even twice a year. Watching someone fail to find what is included teaches more than another brainstorm about brand voice. VoiceOver and larger text settings will also reveal whether your "fine print" is actually readable. Treat those failures as copy bugs, not edge-case trivia.',
      'Assume many sessions will be interrupted by messages, maps, or payment apps mid-scroll. Put irreversible facts — final sale, lead times, allergens — high enough that a distracted buyer still absorbs them. Clarity on the small screen protects conversion and conscience at once, which is the entire point of mobile-first writing rather than desktop text squeezed downward.',
      'Finally, treat mobile copy review as a release gate equal to desktop design QA. Before any major template change, walk five top SKUs on a real device held in one hand while walking, because that is closer to shopping reality than a resized browser window on a designer laptop. Note where thumbs must stretch, where sticky elements collide with footnotes, and where a sentence that sounded elegant in critique becomes exhausting at thumbnail width. Capture those notes in the merchandising backlog beside conversion bugs. Over a quarter, those small observations compound into a storefront that feels considerate rather than merely compressed from a desktop original.',
      'Accordion labels should name the anxiety, not the department. "Will it fit my 40mm case?" outperforms "Specifications" when thumbs are impatient. Rewrite three labels using phrases copied from support subjects and watch expand rates climb without adding word count inside panels.',
      'Payment-plan disclosures that appear only in a desktop footer vanish for mobile shoppers evaluating affordability. Surface installment eligibility near price with the same honesty as shipping thresholds. Surprise financing fine print after tap-to-pay feels like a bait switch even when legal.',
      'Dark-mode screenshots in QA catch light-gray disclaimers that disappear against charcoal themes. If your brand ships a dark storefront skin, budget a copy pass for contrast, not only a design pass for icons. Invisible constraints equal missing constraints.',
      'Thumb-zone maps matter when sticky bars and chat widgets compete. Walk the page holding the phone at the bottom edge and note which critical links require a grip change. Relocate size-guide entry points into the natural arc rather than arguing shoppers should stretch more carefully.',
    ],
