    content: [
      'Supplier copy is written for catalogs, distributors, and factory checklists — not for your customers staring at a marketplace thumbnail. It often recycles export English, lists certificate codes without context, and appears identically on dozens of reseller pages. Marketplaces punish thin duplication; shoppers punish vagueness. Your job is translation from wholesale language into buyer language that is uniquely yours while remaining factually loyal to the product.',
      'Strip claims you cannot defend before you polish tone. If a supplier PDF announces medical-grade silicone without a standard you can verify, soften to material identity you can stand behind or obtain documentation. Duplicating bold claims you never tested is how listing suspensions and trust crises begin. Courageous deletion is part of rewriting, not a failure of creativity.',
      'Identify the shopper\'s first three questions for the category and answer them early. For a desk lamp that might mean brightness range, bulb type and replacements, clamp versus base, and glare. Supplier feature order usually follows manufacturing chronology — alloy body, then ABS base — which is not buying chronology. Reorder mercilessly around decisions, not around how the factory pack slip was written.',
      'Replace jargon with measurable outcomes while keeping the numbers. Three thousand Kelvin warm white plus a note about softer evening reading light helps more than "premium illumination system." Keep the measurement; add the lived meaning. Shoppers trust instruments more than adjectives, and they trust adjectives more when instruments sit beside them.',
      'Add your operational truth that no factory sheet includes. Lead times from your warehouse, packaging choices, plug types, and your warranty process decide messaging and seller metrics. Supplier copy never mentions how you fulfill, yet those details determine whether the listing feels coherent with tracking emails and review replies later.',
      'Differentiate against identical-source competitors through use-case framing rather than fake exclusivity. The same power bank can be positioned for travel screening limits, photo shoots, or emergency kits if you support the angle with photos and accessories you actually sell. Choose a lane you can prove. Invented uniqueness collapses when a reverse image search finds twenty clones.',
      'Create an intake rewrite checklist: remove competitor names, convert units for your audience, localize spelling, delete empty superlatives, write captions for three original photos, and insert FAQs drawn from your support macros. Run the checklist before go-live. Process beats inspiration when you onboard fifty SKUs from a new vendor in a week.',
      'Batch by product family when volume is high. Rewrite the hero description once, then generate variant-aware openings for capacity, finish, and pack count so each listing is not a clone with a swapped digit. A Bulk Description Rewriter can accelerate first drafts; the checklist still owns quality, and editors still catch illegal leftovers from supplier boilerplate.',
      'Marketplace character limits force ruthless priority. Bullet fields are not the place for brand-origin poems. Lead each bullet with a searchable phrase, then the proof. Save longer story for enhanced brand modules or your DTC page where format allows breathing room without truncating mid-claim.',
      'Keep a side-by-side archive of supplier originals versus your rewrites. When factories update milliamps or certifications, you can spot drift without starting from zero. Spec drift left unchecked recreates not-as-described returns and trains reviewers to assume your store copies blindly.',
      'Certificates can remain when audiences expect them, but each needs a plain-language gloss. Trademark misuse and competitor comparisons sometimes hide in supplier boilerplate — delete them so you do not inherit legal risk. Dual-unit displays such as five hundred milliliters beside fluid ounces reduce wrong-size accessory purchases across regions.',
      'Never paste competitor marketplace text as inspiration into your draft document. Recycled phrasing is detectable and trains bad habits. Originality can still be plain; it simply has to be yours. Close each rewrite sprint by killing one recurring customer confusion theme. Supplier language evolves slowly; your listing should evolve every time buyers teach you what was missing.',
      'Pair rewrite briefs with photography direction. If you claim something fits in a laptop sleeve, shoot it half inside the sleeve. Price-tier language for pro versus lite models must reflect true feature differences after rewrite — do not invent hierarchy hardware cannot support merely to fill a bullet quota. Measured specifics are harder for clone sellers to steal meaningfully.',
      'Think of marketplace rewriting as ongoing product education for strangers who cannot touch the item before purchase. Each week, pick one high-return SKU and ask support for the three newest confusion phrases customers used. Fold those phrases into bullets and FAQs, then re-check that your photos still prove the updated sentences. Compare your listing monthly against top competitors not to copy them, but to confirm your measured dimensions, accessory lists, and fulfillment promises remain visibly more useful. Use-case originality plus operational specificity is a durable moat even when the hardware inside the box is shared with other sellers. When a vendor sends a new PDF, schedule a sixty-minute rewrite appointment on the calendar instead of pasting and promising to clean it later — later is how duplicate content and stale specs become permanent.',
      'Translate certificate alphabet soup into shopper stakes. "IP67" becomes protection against brief immersion during sink rinses, with a reminder it is not an invitation to swim. The code can stay; the living translation earns the bullet.',
      'Watch for imperial and metric collisions inside a single supplier paragraph. Mixing 12 in handles with 30 cm bodies without labeling either system guarantees wrong accessory orders. Normalize to your audience and parenthesize the alternate once.',
      'When a factory ships revised wattage mid-season, freeze ad creative that cites the old number the same afternoon you rewrite the listing. Paid traffic against stale proof burns refunds faster than organic ever could. Treat creative and listing as one update ticket.',
      'Build bilingual glossaries for recurring components if you sell into multiple language storefronts. Machine-translated "clip" versus "clamp" errors recreate support load you just eradicated in English. Human-verify the ten nouns buyers search most.',
    ],
